Simple. Add the function. Note: "all bookmarks whose tags include every tag in `tags
`". AND semantics: bookmarks that have all tags. Empty tags → all bookmarks.

Implementation:

```ts
export function filterByTags(bookmarks: Bookmark[], tags: string[]): Bookmark[] {

  if (tags.length === 0) return bookmarks;
  return bookmarks.filter((b) => tags.every((t) => b.tags.includes(t)));
}
```

Actually, `tags.every(...)` already returns true when empty, so it naturally returns
 all bookmarks — though returning the same array or a filtered copy are equivalent
. Either is fine. For clarity, I'll keep the simple every form; the spec says "return
 all bookmarks unchanged" — even if the filter passes through everything, it becomes
 a new array with the same elements. The safest is the explicit early return.

